        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: MCP Apps (ui://) Phase 0 — generic host in Control UI + plugin-declared MCP servers",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds Phase 0 support for the MCP Apps extension (`io.modelcontextprotocol/ui`) to OpenClaw's generic bundle MCP client and Control UI. Any configured MCP server can bind a tool to a `ui://` resource through `_meta.ui.resourceUri`; no server-specific host integration is required.\n\nThe official Excalidraw MCP App server is the conformance target. The external proof-of-concept plugin remains out of tree at [fuller-stack-dev/openclaw-excalidraw-plugin](https://github.com/fuller-stack-dev/openclaw-excalidraw-plugin).\n\n## What changed\n\n### Generic MCP Apps runtime\n\n- Declares the `io.modelcontextprotocol/ui` client capability for `text/html;profile=mcp-app`.\n- Parses nested and legacy flat `_meta.ui` declarations and reads bound `ui://` resources after successful tool calls.\n- Keeps additive app resource reads outside the MCP server tool/utility circuit breaker, so a missing preview cannot pause otherwise-working tools.\n- Filters app-only tools (`_meta.ui.visibility` without `model`) from agent exposure.\n- Preserves the normal text/structured tool result for non-UI surfaces.\n\n### Durable, bounded view storage\n\n- Stores complete app view snapshots outside transcripts in the shared OpenClaw state SQLite database.\n- Persists only an opaque, validated descriptor in `details.mcpApp`; app HTML, tool input, and tool result do not inflate `chat.history`.\n- Enforces 30-day retention, 128 views, 256 MiB total, and 8 MiB per payload, including all persisted string fields.\n- Physically deletes expired rows during lookup and prunes oldest rows on insertion.\n- Invalid, expired, or evicted descriptors fail closed.\n\n### Production Control UI host\n\n- Resolves descriptors through the existing authenticated Control UI read flow; public index HTML contains no app-resource capability.\n- Sends the live device/token/password credential in the same-origin resource request and retries unresolved previews after credential rotation.\n- Uses a double-frame host: an opaque-origin outer proxy and a nested `allow-scripts allow-same-origin` app frame.\n- Applies the app-declared CSP in the proxy response and does not delegate camera, microphone, geolocation, or clipboard permissions.\n- Renders the app inline even while the enclosing tool disclosure is collapsed.\n- Defers hydration until a preview approaches the viewport, retains up to eight activated frames, and pauses the least-recent retained frame with an explicit reload affordance.\n- Keeps activated iframe state stable across tool-detail toggles and ordinary chat rerenders.\n- Survives Gateway restart plus page reload without rerunning the MCP tool.\n\n### Plugin surface\n\n- Native plugin manifests can declare `mcpServers`, merged through the enabled-bundle path.\n- User configuration wins by server name; disabling a plugin detaches its declared server.\n\n## Security model\n\n- App documents and MCP metadata remain untrusted input.\n- Resource lookup requires normal authenticated Control UI read access plus an unguessable 192-bit view ID.\n- The outer sandbox has an opaque origin; the inner same-origin capability is confined to the proxy document.\n- App CSP origins reject control characters and are validated before response-header construction.\n- App-initiated host requests such as `tools/call` and `ui/open-link` receive JSON-RPC method-not-found in Phase 0.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Live Rosita/Tailscale proof with the real Excalidraw MCP App: created the OpenClaw plugin architecture diagram, kept it visible under a collapsed tool row, restarted the Gateway, reloaded the existing conversation, and recovered the same editable app without rerunning the tool.\n- Live `chat.history` proof: the persisted MCP App descriptor serialized to 149 bytes and contained only `viewId`, `serverName`, `toolName`, and `resourceUri`.\n- AWS Crabbox (`cbx_100691b2e58d`, `c7a.8xlarge`):\n  - [436 focused tests passed](https://crabbox.openclaw.ai/portal/runs/run_36e1d9a26aaa)\n  - [storage retention/bounds tests passed](https://crabbox.openclaw.ai/portal/runs/run_3440977bb7b5)\n  - [Chromium MCP App E2E passed](https://crabbox.openclaw.ai/portal/runs/run_fb2774cc9f5c)\n- Current refreshed head:\n  - 14 materialization assertions\n  - 44 MCP runtime assertions\n  - 368 Gateway/HTTP assertions\n  - 46 UI assertions\n  - Chromium sandbox E2E\n  - `pnpm check` and `git diff --check`\n  - focused uncommitted `gpt-5.6-sol` autoreview: clean, no actionable findings\n\n## Maintainer decisions before merge\n\n1. **Sandbox origin:** the stable MCP Apps contract requires the Host and Sandbox to use different origins and grants the Sandbox `allow-scripts allow-same-origin`. The current same-origin route intentionally keeps the outer proxy opaque; adding `allow-same-origin` there would expose the Control UI origin. A genuinely distinct sandbox origin (or an explicit experimental/non-conformant scope) needs owner direction.\n2. **Plugin API:** approve or remove the new public native-plugin `mcpServers` manifest contract.\n\n## Current limit\n\nPhase 0 is view-only for app-to-host calls. A full host bridge for app-initiated tool calls and navigation remains follow-up work. Until a distinct sandbox origin is provided, origin-dependent app behavior such as localStorage, IndexedDB, normal CORS origins, and OAuth is not conformant.\n\n## Release-note context\n\nThe Control UI can render and restore generic MCP App tool interfaces while keeping large app payloads out of chat transcripts; standards-complete origin behavior remains gated on the sandbox-origin decision above.\n",
        "branch": "feat/mcp-apps-phase0-excalidraw-poc",
